.TH M4 1
.SH NAME
m4 \- macro processor, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B m4
[
.B -e
.I script
]
[
.B -f
.I scriptfile
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I M4
is a Limbo-native workalike for a bounded subset of
.IR m4 (1),
the macro processor, compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I M4
reads a program (its input text, with macro calls expanded in place) from
.I file
arguments (concatenated in order), or standard input if none are given,
or from
.B -e
.I script
and
.B -f
.I scriptfile
arguments (repeatable, evaluated before any files), and writes the
expanded result to standard output.
.PP
Ordinary text is copied through unchanged. A run of letters, digits and
underscores starting with a letter or underscore is a potential macro
name; if it names a defined macro, it is replaced by that macro's
expansion, which is itself rescanned for further macro calls before
being emitted \(em the same rescanning real
.IR m4 (1)
does, including on a macro's own stored body at every later invocation,
not only on freshly-read text.
.PP
A name is invoked with arguments only if
.B (
follows it immediately, with no intervening space; arguments are
separated by top-level commas (not ones inside nested parentheses or
quotes) and are themselves expanded before being substituted into the
macro's body. A bare name with no parentheses has zero arguments; an
explicit
.B ()
has exactly one, the empty string \(em these differ in
.B $#
even though
.B $1
is the empty string either way.
.PP
Quoting uses the default
.B ` \&...\ '
pair (backtick / apostrophe), nested (an inner
.RB ` ... '
pair keeps its own markers literally in the output; only the outermost
pair is stripped) and suppresses macro scanning inside. A
.B #
starts a comment that runs to end of line and is copied to the output
verbatim, without any macro expansion inside it \(em this is
.IR m4 's
real default behavior, not a bounded-scope simplification, and it is
easy to trip over: an unquoted
.B #
inside an argument swallows the rest of the line, including whatever
would have closed the argument list.
.SS Builtins
.TP
.BI define( name , body )
Define
.I name
to expand to
.I body
on every later invocation, with
.BR $1 through
.BR $9 ,
.BR $0 ,
and
.B $#
in
.I body
substituted at call time (never at define time) with that call's
arguments, the macro's own name, and the argument count.
.TP
.BI undefine( name )
Remove
.IR name 's
definition; it is no longer recognized as a macro.
.TP
.BI ifdef( name , then , [ else ])
.I then
if
.I name
is currently defined,
.I else
(empty if omitted) otherwise.
.TP
.BI ifelse( a , b , then " [, " a2 , b2 , then2 ", ... ] [, " default ])
Compares
.I a
to
.IR b ;
if equal, the result is
.IR then .
Otherwise, if a further
.IR a2 , b2 , then2
triple is given, repeats the comparison with it, and so on; if nothing
matches and a trailing, un-tripled argument remains, that is the
default result.
.TP
.BI include( filename )
Reads
.I filename
and rescans its content as more input.
.TP
.B dnl
Discards everything from itself to the end of the line, including the
newline \(em unlike other builtins, it does not look for a following
.B (
at all.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/m4.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR m4test (1)
.SH BUGS
This is a deliberately bounded subset. No
.BR divert / undivert
(output diversion), no
.BR changequote / changecom
(the quote and comment delimiters are always the defaults above), no
.BR syscmd / esyscmd
(shelling out), no
.B eval
(arithmetic expressions \(em a genuinely common builtin in real
.IR m4 ,
left out because this bounded scope has no expression evaluator; see
.IR dc (1)
for a separate calculator), no
.BR len / substr / index / translit / format ,
no
.BR pushdef / popdef
(macro definition stacks \(em
.B define
always simply overwrites).
.PP
.BR define ", " undefine ", " ifdef ", " ifelse ", " include ", and " dnl
are always recognized as builtins and can never be redefined or
undefined, unlike real
.IR m4 ,
where they are just regular (if predefined) macros like any other.
.PP
Every rule about argument collection, quoting, and comments above was
checked against the host's own
.I m4
with targeted probes before any of this was written, not read off the
.I m4
manual from memory \(em including the default
.B #
comment behavior, found only because an early test program with an
unquoted
.B #
in it produced a real oracle error
.RB ( "end of file in argument list" )
that had to be tracked down rather than assumed away.
